    "@id" : "_:genid3988"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition" : [ {
    "@value" : "Order in which a branch-following sweep (SweepSeeding.from_previous) traverses its axis. Ignored for independent seeding, where order is irrelevant."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo"
  } ],
  "https://w3id.org/linkml/permissible_values" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepDirection#bidirectional"
  }, {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepDirection#down"
  }, {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepDirection#up"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepDirection#bidirectional",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "bidirectional (SweepDirection)"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepDirection"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition" : [ {
    "@value" : "Increase then decrease over the same values, recording both branches. Where the two branches differ, the sweep traces a hysteresis loop that exposes coexisting stable states (bistability / multistability). The multivalued generalisation of Continuation.bothside."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation" : [ {
    "@value" : "bidirectional"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepDirection#down",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "down (SweepDirection)"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepDirection"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition" : [ {
    "@value" : "Decrease the parameter monotonically (high to low), once."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation" : [ {
    "@value" : "down"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepDirection#up",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "up (SweepDirection)"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepDirection"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition" : [ {
    "@value" : "Increase the parameter monotonically (low to high), once."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation" : [ {
    "@value" : "up"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepSeeding",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "SweepSeeding"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#equivalentClass" : [ {
    "@id" : "_:genid3992"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition" : [ {
    "@value" : "How each point of a parameter sweep (Exploration) obtains its initial state. Determines whether the sweep points are independent (and therefore parallelisable) or whether the trajectory follows a solution branch as the swept parameter is changed quasi-statically. The latter is forward-time-integration branch following — distinct from numerical continuation of equilibria (the Continuation class), which tracks branches with a Newton corrector rather than by simulation."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo"
  } ],
  "https://w3id.org/linkml/permissible_values" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepSeeding#from_previous"
  }, {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepSeeding#independent"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepSeeding#from_previous",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "from_previous (SweepSeeding)"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepSeeding"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition" : [ {
    "@value" : "Each point is seeded from the final state of the preceding point, so the trajectory follows the solution branch as the swept parameter changes quasi-statically. Forces sequential, order-dependent execution (emitted as a scan, not a vmap). Used to reach a working point by slowly ramping a parameter (e.g. global coupling) and, with sweep_direction, to expose hysteresis / multistability. The sweep-granularity form of InitialStateMethod.from_working_point, which ramps a parameter to one working point."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation" : [ {
    "@value" : "from_previous"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepSeeding#independent",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "independent (SweepSeeding)"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SweepSeeding"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition" : [ {
    "@value" : "Points are order-independent and may be evaluated in parallel (see parallel_mode). Default. Independent of each OTHER, which is not the same as each cold-starting: where the experiment declares a transient, the settle is integrated once and every point opens from the state it reached, so what is shared between points is a settled state rather than a running trajectory. That is sound while the swept parameter does not change the approach, and unsound wherever the sweep crosses a bifurcation, since a point then inherits an attractor it would not have reached on its own. The emitter integrates one settle, not one per point, so the name promises independence between points, not a cold start at each."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation" : [ {
    "@value" : "independent"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/SystemType",
