  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "functions (slot)"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation" : [ {
    "@value" : "functions"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/functions/FCCorrelation",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/description" : [ {
    "@value" : "The Pearson correlation between the off-diagonal upper triangles of two functional connectivity matrices, the usual measure of how well a simulated FC matches an empirical one. The diagonal is left out because every correlation matrix carries the same unit diagonal, and the lower triangle because it mirrors the upper, so neither adds agreement."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/Function"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "FCCorrelation"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/functions/FalseDiscoveryControl",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/description" : [ {
    "@value" : "The p-values of a family of tests adjusted to control the false discovery rate, by the Benjamini-Hochberg step-up procedure or the Benjamini-Yekutieli one, as scipy.stats.false_discovery_control adjusts them, and the tests kept at a given rate. The entries of a family that are not NaN are its tests. A NaN is no test; it stays NaN, is never selected and is not counted, where scipy refuses it. Any other entry outside 0 to 1 is refused, as scipy refuses it. It returns p_adjusted, shaped and labelled as p, and n_tests; with a rate q also selected, true for every test up to the largest p-value at or below q times its rank over the number of tests, and n_selected."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/Function"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "FalseDiscoveryControl"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/functions/GroupedCorrelation",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/description" : [ {
    "@value" : "The units of one dimension are cut into groups by a score, each group is reduced to the mean of its members' x and y, and x is correlated with y over the group means; the same correlation over single units is returned beside it. Groups are bins of equal width over the grouping variable's range (a bin no unit falls in stays empty) or runs of equal size along its order, and the grouping variable is x unless another is named. A unit enters where x, y and the grouping variable are all finite, labelled inputs are paired on the labels of the dimension, each of which occurs once, and every other dimension an input carries is kept, with one result per element of it. Per group on `group` it returns x_mean, y_mean, y_sd, y_se and size; over the group means the least-squares slope and intercept, Pearson r and two-sided p, and the line at the smallest and largest x_mean as fit_x and fit_y on `end`; over units r_unit and p_unit; and the counts n_nonempty_groups, n_units and n_units_total. A correlation is NaN with fewer than three points or a constant side. With more groups than units, runs of equal size give each unit a group of its own and leave the last groups empty."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/Function"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "GroupedCorrelation"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/functions/Histogram",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/description" : [ {
    "@value" : "The number of values in each of n_bins bins of equal width from lower to upper, each bin closed below and open above and the last closed on both sides, as numpy.histogram cuts them. Every finite value is counted, whatever its dimensions, a value outside the range is not, and a mask restricts the count to the entries it marks. It returns count and center on `bin`, edge on `bin_edge`, the bins' width, and n, the number of values counted."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/Function"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "Histogram"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/functions/SetCosine",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/description" : [ {
    "@value" : "The cosine similarity between a per-node value and a set's membership vector, which measures how much of the value lies on the set. It is 1 when the value is proportional to the membership, which for a 0/1 membership means uniform on the members and zero elsewhere, and 0 when the value is zero on every member. Scaling the value by a positive factor leaves it unchanged, so it reads the pattern of a response and not its size."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/Function"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "SetCosine"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/functions/SetMean",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/description" : [ {
    "@value" : "The mean of a per-node value over a set of nodes, the set given as a per-node membership that is 1 for a member and 0 otherwise, or a non-negative weight. For a 0/1 membership it is the plain mean over the members; for weights it is the weighted mean."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/Function"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "SetMean"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/functions/SoftPeakFrequency",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/description" : [ {
    "@value" : "A differentiable peak frequency of one spectrum. The frequencies are averaged with softmax weights over the power, normalised to its maximum and sharpened by beta, so the result approaches the frequency of the largest peak as beta grows while keeping a gradient with respect to the spectrum. The leading skip bins are left out, which drops the DC bin and the slow drift a finite record leaves at the lowest frequencies. The normalised power peaks at exactly 1, so the weights are written as exp(beta * (power / max(power) - 1)), the max-shifted softmax, which cannot overflow. The function takes one spectrum; a pipeline step applies it per node with apply_on_dimension."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/Function"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "SoftPeakFrequency"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/functions/WelchPSD",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual" ],
  "http://purl.org/dc/terms/description" : [ {
    "@value" : "Welch's estimate of the power spectral density of a signal along its leading (time) axis, one-sided and scaled as power per unit frequency, with a periodic Hann window and each segment's mean removed, which are the defaults of scipy.signal.welch. Segments of nperseg samples start every nperseg - noverlap samples, a trailing remainder is dropped, each segment is zero-padded to nfft samples, and the segments' periodograms are averaged. The result has nfft // 2 + 1 rows, at the frequencies rfftfreq(nfft, 1 / fs), and the signal's trailing axes. nperseg, noverlap and nfft are integers fixed when the code is generated, because they set the shape of the result; they cannot be floats, traced values or the axes of a sweep."
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/Function"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label" : [ {
    "@value" : "WelchPSD"
  } ]
}, {
  "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/gather",
  "@type" : [ "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty", "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty" ],
  "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy" : [ {
    "@id" : "https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl"
  } ],
